
********** I. INTRODUCTION

THERE NOW EXISTS A DISPLAY SLAVE FOR THE COLOR SCOPE, WHICH RUNS
ON THE PDP-6 (AND HOPEFULLY ON THE PDP-10 IF NECESSARY). IT PROVIDES
BASIC CAPABILITIES FOR DRIVING THE SCOPE, MAINTAINING PROPER TIMING,
AND MANAGING DISPLAY ITEMS.


********** II. THE COLOR SCOPE

THE COLOR SCOPE ITSELF IS DESCRIBED IN HARDWARE MEMO #6. A BRIEF
RUNDOWN IS PROVIDED HERE:
THE SCREEN OF THE COLOR SCOPE IS NINE BITS EACH WAY, THEREFORE
PROVIDING HALF THE RESOLUTION OF THE 340. THE SCREEN IS
ADDRESSED BY EIGHTEEN BITS: TWO NINE-BIT BYTES <X,Y>. <0,0>
IS THE LOWER LEFT-HAND CORNER. IT CAN ONLY DISPLAY
RANDOM POINTS; THAT IS, THE HARDWARE DOES NOT PROVIDE VECTOR
CAPABILITY. SINCE POINTS CAN ONLY BE DISPLAYED AT A RATE OF 1 EVERY
210. MICROSECONDS, ONLY ABOUT 400. POINTS CAN BE DISPLAYED WITHOUT
UNREASONABLE FLICKER. THE COLOR USED TO DISPLAY A POINT IS CONTROLLED
BY FIFTEEN COLOR BITS; THESE ARE PACKED IN AN EIGHTEEN-BIT PDP-10
HALFWORD AS THREE SIX-BIT BYTES, WHICH SPECIFY RED, GREEN,
AND BLUE INTENSITIES. WITHIN EACH BYTE, THE HIGH BIT IS NOT
APPLICABLE TO COLOR (USED FOR SOMETHING ELSE), THE NEXT BIT
IS AN ENABLE BIT, AND THE LOW FOUR SPECIFY THE INTENSITY FOR
THAT COLOR. COMBINING THESE THREE COLORS IN VARIOUS INTENSITIES
PROVIDES A FULL SPECTRUM. SOME USEFUL STANDARD VALUES:
	WHITE	313733		PINK	312525
	MAGENTA	370037		RED	370000
	ORANGE	372700		YELLOW	363600
	YELGRN	313700		GREEN	003700
	BLUGRN	003730		BLUE	000037
	VIOLET	270037		BLACK	000000
FROM THIS POINT ON, THE WORD "COLOR" WILL GENERALLY MEAN
AN EIGHTEEN-BIT QUANTITY SPECIFYING COLOR SCOPE INTENSITIES
AS DEFINED ABOVE.


********** III. THE COLOR SLAVE

THE COLOR SLAVE, ONCE LOADED, SHOULD BE STARTED AT LOCATION 102.
ONCE STARTED, IT ZEROS OUT LOCATION 100 (WHICH IS INITIALLY -1) TO
INDICATE THAT IT IS ALIVE. FROM THIS POINT ON IT IS READY TO
RECEIVE COMMANDS.
COMMANDS ARE GIVEN TO THE SLAVE BY DEPOSITING A NON-ZERO WORD IN
LOCATION 100. WHEN THE SLAVE NOTICES THAT LOCATION 100 HAS GONE
NON-ZERO IT WILL PROCESS THE COMMAND, DEPOSIT A RETURN VALUE IN
LOCATION 101, THEN ZERO LOCATION 100. THE RETURN VALUE WILL BE
NON-ZERO; THUS IF THE MASTER ZEROS OUT 101, DEPOSITS THE COMMAND
IN 100, THEN EXAMINES 101 AFTER SOME REASONABLE TIME PERIOD,
FINDING A ZERO INDICATES THAT THE SLAVE IS DEAD. ANOTHER CONVENTION
IS THAT POSITIVE RETURN VALUES INDICATE THAT THE SLAVE IS HAPPY,
WHILE NEGATIVE RETURN VALUES INDICATE FAILURE OF SOME KIND; THEESE
ARE DOCUMENTED BELOW.
THE COLOR SLAVE MAINTAINS A LIST OF DISPLAY ITEMS IN A BUFFER;
NEW ITEMS ARE ALWAYS ADDED TO THE END OF THE BUFFER, AND AS ITEMS ARE
DELETED THE BUFFER IS COMPACTED DOWNWARD. THIS HAS SOME USEFUL
CONSEQUENCES WHICH WILL BE NOTED LATER.
NOTE FROM THE ABOVE DESCRIPTION THAT A SLAVE COMMAND CONSISTS OF
A SINGLE NON-ZERO THIRTY-SIX BIT WORD. THERE ARE TWO KINDS OF
COMMANDS WHICH CAN BE GIVEN TO THE COLOR SLAVE: NEW DISPLAY ITEMS
TO BE ADDED TO THE DISPLAY BUFFER, AND RANDOM COMMANDS WHICH DO
VARIOUS RANDOM THINGS. A SPECIAL CASE IS THE COMMAND -1: THIS MEANS
FLUSH ALL DISPLAY ITEMS AND REINITIALIZE THE SLAVE; IT RETURNS 
THE VALUE 1 IN LOCATION 101.

THE FIRST TYPE OF COMMAND (NEW DISPLAY ITEM) HAS 1 IN BIT 4.9
(THE SIGN BIT). THE LEFT HALF OF THE WORD CONTAINS THE COLOR IN
WHICH TO DISPLAY THE ITEM (EXCEPT THAT, AS A SPECIAL CROCK, THE
COLOR 070707 MEANS USE THE SAME COLOR AS THE PREVIOUS ITEM);
OF THE THREE UNUSED BITS, THE SIGN BIT IS AS DESCRIBED, AND
THE OTHER TWO (THE 4000 AND 40 BITS) DETERMINE THE TYPE OF
THE ITEM AS FOLLOWS:
	0000	ABSOLUTE POINT. THE RIGHT HALF OF THE WORD IS DIVIDED
		INTO TWO NINE-BIT BYTES; THE HIGH-ORDER BYTE IS THE
		X-COORDINATE, THE LOW-ORDER BYTE THE Y-COORDINATE.
		THE DISPLAY CURSOR IS MOVED TO THAT ABSOLUTE SCOPE
		POSITION AND A POINT DISPLAYED THERE IN THE
		SPECIFIED COLOR.
	0040	RELATIVE POINT. THE RIGHT HALF OF THE WORD IS
		TWO NINE-BIT BYTES, THE HIGH-ORDER BEING A SIGNED
		DELTA-X AND THE LOW-ORDER A SIGNED DELTA-Y. THE
		POSITION OF THE DISPLAY CURSOR IS INCREMENTED BY
		DELTA-X/DELTA-Y AND A POINT DISPLAYED AT THE NEW
		SCOPE POSITION. (IF THE RESULT OF INCREMENTING
		THE DISPLAY CURSOR EXCEEDS NINE BITS, IT WRAPS
		AROUND IN THE USUAL MANNER.)
	4000	RELATIVE VECTOR. DRAW A VECTOR AS SPECIFIED BY
		DELTA-X AND DELTA-Y. A POINT IS DISPLAYED AT THE
		CURRENT POSITION, AT THE END POSITION (AS FOR A
		RELATIVE POINT), AND AT INTERMEDIATE POINTS, SPACED
		APART BY A DENSITY FACTOR (WHICH INITIALLY IS FOUR
		POINTS APART BUT MAY BE ALTERED; SEE BELOW).
	4040	CHARACTER. THE RIGHT HALF OF THE COMMAND IS
		INTERPRETED AS FOLLOWS:
			BITS 1.1-1.7	ASCII CODE FOR CHARACTER
			BITS 1.8-1.9	IGNORED
			BITS 2.1-2.4	SIZE
			BITS 2.5-2.7	IGNORED
			BITS 2.8-2.8	SPACE-AFTER
		THE CHARACTER SPECIFIED IS DISPLAYED AS A 5*7 DOT
		MATRIX AT THE CURRENT CURSOR POSITION. THE SIZE+1
		INDICATES HOW FAR APART POINTS OF THE MATRIX ARE:
		SIZE 0 PLACES THEM 1 POINT APART, ETC.
		THE SPACE-AFTER FIELD IS DECODED AS FOLLOWS:
		0 MEANS SPACE FORWARD (NORMAL), I.E. MOVE THE
		CURSOR 8*<SIZE+1> POINTS TO THE RIGHT.
		1 MEANS LINEFEED-AFTER, I.E. MOVE THE CURSOR 
		12.*<SIZE+1> POINTS DOWNWARD (GOOD FOR VERTICAL TEXT).
		2 OR 3 MEANS DO NOT MOVE THE CURSOR AFTER DISPLAYING
		THE CHARACTER.
		SOME CHARACTERS ARE HANDLED IN A SPECIAL MANNER
		AND IGNORE THE SPACE-AFTER FIELD:
		^H DOES A BACKSPACE, AS USUAL.
		^I DOES A TAB (ADVANCES CURSOR TO NEXT MULTIPLE-OF-8
			CHARACTER POSITION).
		^J IS LINE FEED, AS USUAL.
		^K IS LINE STARVE.
		^L IS HOME UP (SETS CURSOR POSITION TO 000700).
		^M IS CARRIAGE RETURN (SET X-COORDINATE OF 
			DISPLAY CURSOR TO ZERO).
		ALL OTHER CONTROL CHARACTERS DISPLAY NOTHING AND
		DO NOT MOVE THE CURSOR.
		THE RUBOUT CODE (177) IS LIKE ANY OTHER PRINTING
		CHARACTER, AND DISPLAYS AS A SOLID RECTANGLE.
		THE IGNORED BITS MENTIONED ABOVE SHOULD BE ZERO
		TO AVOID GIVING THE -1 COMMAND INADVERTENTLY.
COMMANDS WHICH ADD NEW DISPLAY ITEMS MAY FAIL ONLY IF THE DISPLAY
BUFFER IS FULL; IN THIS CASE THE SLAVE RETURNS -2. OTHERWISE IT
RETURNS A POSITIVE NON-ZERO ITEM NUMBER, BY WHICH OTHER COMMANDS
MAY REFER TO THE NEWLY-CREATED DISPLAY ITEM.

THE SECOND TYPE OF COMMAND (VARIOUS RANDOM HACKS) HAVE THE SIGN
BIT ZERO. THEY ARE DECODED AS FOLLOWS:
	BITS 4.8-4.4	COMMAND NUMBER
	BITS 4.3-3.1	ITEM NUMBER (NOT NECESSARILY USED BY COMMAND)
	BITS 2.9-1.1	COMMAND DATA (NOT NECESSARILY USED BY COMMAND)
THE FOLLOWING RULES APPLY TO ALL OF THESE COMMANDS:
IF THE COMMAND NUMBER IS 0 OR GREATER THAN THE LARGEST LEGAL
COMMAND NUMBER, -3 IS RETURNED.
COMMANDS WHICH REFER TO SOME DISPLAY ITEM SPECIFIED BY THE ITEM
NUMBER RETURN -2 FOR AN ILLEGAL ITEM NUMBER (0 OR TOO LARGE),
OR -1 FOR A LEGAL ITEM NUMBER FOR WHICH THE ITEM HAS BEEN
DELETED OR NEVER EXISTED.
COMMANDS WHICH RETURN SOME DATUM RETURN IT AS AN EIGHTEEN-BIT NUMBER
IN THE RIGHT HALF OF A WORD, WITH THE 4.8 BIT TURNED ON IN THE
LEFT HALF TO GUARANTEE THAT IT WILL BE POSITIVE AND NON-ZERO.
COMMANDS WHICH DO NOT RETURN A DATUM RETURN 1 IF THEY SUCCEED.
THERE ARE PRESENTLY SEVEN RANDOM COMMANDS:
	1	GET POSITION. RETURNS THE RIGHT HALF OF THE
		ITEM SPECIFIED BY THE ITEM NUMBER. COMMAND DATA
		IS IGNORED.
	2	GET COLOR. RETURNS THE COLOR OF THE SPECIFIED
		ITEM; THE 404040 BITS ARE SET TO ZERO.
		IGNORES COMMAND DATA.
	3	GET DENSITY. IGNORES ITEM NUMBER AND COMMAND DATA.
		RETURNS THE CURRENT DENSITY FACTOR USED FOR
		DRAWING VECTORS.
	4	DELETE ITEM. DELETE THE SPECIFIED ITEM FROM
		THE DISPLAY BUFFER. RETURNS 1. COMMAND DATA IS
		IGNORED.
	5	SET POSITION. CLOBBER THE RIGHT HALF OF THE SPECIFIED
		DISPLAY ITEM WITH THE COMMAND DATA. RETUNS 1.
		ONE USEFUL APPLICATION OF THIS IS TO CREATE
		AN ABSOLUTE POINT FOLLOWED BY SEVERAL RELATIVE
		ITEMS; THEN THE RELATIVE ITEMS CAN BE MOVED AROUND
		THE SCREEN BY CLOBBERING THE POSITION OF
		THE ABSOLUTE POINT.
	6	SET COLOR. CLOBBER THE COLOR OF THE SPECIFIED
		ITEM WITH THE COMMAND DATA. THE 404040 BITS OF
		THE COMMAND DATA ARE IGNORED; THUS THIS COMMAND
		CANNOT CHANGE A VECTOR TO A CHARACTER OR ANYTHING
		LIKE THAT. RETURNS 1.
		IF ONE CREATES AN ITEM WITH SOME COLOR, AND SUCCESSIVE
		ITEMS ARE OF COLOR 070707, THEN CLOBBERING THE COLOR
		OF THE FIRST ITEM CHANGES THE COLOR OF ALL THE
		SUCCEEDING ITEMS.
	7	SET DENSITY. SET THE DENSITY FACTOR
		USED FOR DRAWING VECTORS. RETURNS -2 IF THE
		COMMAND DATA IS NOT A VALID DENSITY NUMBER (ZERO
		OR GREATER THAN 20); OTHERWISE RETURNS 1.
		NOTE THAT THIS IS A GLOBAL DENSITY FACTOR AND
		AFFECTS ALL VECTORS. THE ITEM NUMBER IS IGNORED.
THE COLOR SLAVE LIVES ON SYS:ATSIGN CSLAVE (ACTUALLY THAT IS A LINK
TO GLS;CSLAVE BIN). IT IS POSSIBLE TO ASSEMBLE A COLOR SLAVE WHICH
ACTUALLY RUNS ON THE 340, IGNORING COLOR SPECIFICATIONS ON
OUTPUT; THIS IS USED FOR DEBUGGING AND WHEN THE COLOR SCOPE IS DOWN.
OF INTEREST IS THE FACT THAT THE NORMAL VERSION TAKES CARE WHEN
CONO'ING COLORS TO THE COLOR SCOPE TO ENABLE THE 40 BIT AND DISABLE
THE 404000 BITS; THUS THE BANK OF 36. SWITCHES IN THE COLOR SCOPE
ROOM WILL ALWAYS BE ENABLED FOR READING WHEN THE COLOR SLAVE
IS RUNNING. THIS IS NOT TRUE OF THE 340 VERSION.


********** IV. THE LISP COLOR MASTER

THE COLOR SLAVE MAY BE USED FROM LISP BY LOADING A LISP WITH SYMBOLS
(TYPE LISP$^K AT DDT), THEN UREADING IN GLS;CMASTR >.
THIS IS WRITTEN IN LAP AND CONSISTS OF THREE FUNCTIONS FOR MANIPULATING
THE COLOR SLAVE.
THE FIRST FUNCTION, COLOR-OPEN, TAKES NO ARGUMENTS AND ATTEMPTS
TO GRAB THE PDP-6. IF IT FAILS IT WILL CREATE AN INFERIOR ON THE
PDP-10. IN EITHER CASE IT LOADS THE SLAVE INTO THE APPROPRIATE
PLACE AND ATTEMPTS TO START IT UP. COLOR-OPEN RETURNS A NUMBER
DESCRIBING WHAT HAPPENED:
	1	GRABBED THE PDP-6, SLAVE IS ALIVE AND WELL.
	0	USED THE PDP-10, THE SLAVE IS ALIVE AND SLOW. (SIGH)
	-1	FAILED TO GET PDP-6 OR PDP-10!
	-2	FAILED TO FIND SYS:ATSIGN CSLAVE
	-3	FAILED WHILE LOADING SYS:ATSIGN CSLAVE
	-4	GRABBED PDP-6, SLAVE REFUSES TO START UP
	-5	GRABBED PDP-10, SLAVE REFUSES TO START UP
IF COLOR-OPEN FAILS, IT CLEANS UP AFTER ITSELF BEFORE RETURNING;
I.E. IT DOESN'T LEAVE LOOSE SUBJOBS OR PDP-6'S LYING AROUND.
IF THE SLAVE IS ALREADY OPENED WHEN COLOR-OPEN IS CALLED, IT IS
CLOSED FIRST AND THEN RE-OPENED.
COLOR-CLOSE TAKES NO ARGUMENTS AND CLOSES THE SLAVE IF IT WAS
OPEN. IF THE PDP-6 WAS BEING USED, ITS MEMORY IS ZEROED BEFORE
CLOSING THE SLAVE. COLOR-CLOSE RETURNS T IF THE SLAVE WAS OPEN,
AND OTHERWISE NIL.
THE THIRD FUNCTION, COLOR-FROB, TAKES ONE ARGUMENT, WHICH SHOULD
BE A NON-ZERO FIXNUM (IF IT ISN'T, A WRNG-TYPE-ARG ERROR BREAK
OCCURS). COLOR-FROB HANDS THE NUMBER TO THE SLAVE, WAITS AN
APPROPRIATE INTERVAL OF TIME, THEN RETURNS AS A FIXNUM WHATEVER
THE SLAVE RETURNED. IF THIS VALUE IS ZERO, IT INDICATES THAT THE
SLAVE TIMED OUT, I.E. MAY BE DEAD. IF THE SLAVE IS NOT OPEN, COLOR-FROB
GIVES A FAIL-ACT ERROR BREAK.
COLOR-OPEN IS ALSO USED AS A FREE VARIABLE TO INDICATE THE STATE
OF THE SLAVE, AND SHOULD NOT BE MANGLED BY LOSERS. THE VALUE T
MEANS THE PDP-6 IS OPEN; NIL, THE PDP-10; UNBOUND, NEITHER.


